== To the Reader

#smallcaps[It] was with no small difficulty that the author’s youth and modesty were persuaded to let him appear as a preacher at our public lecture, and afterward to give us a copy of his sermon, at the request of several ministers and others who heard it. But we quickly found him to be a workman who need not be ashamed before his brethren. So our satisfaction was all the greater to see him choose so noble a #emph[subject,] and treat it with as much strength and clearness as the judicious reader will see in the following composition.

It is a subject which secures to God his great design in the work of redeeming fallen man by the Lord Jesus Christ. That work is plainly so laid out that the glory of the whole should return to him, the blessed one who ordained it, purchased it and applies it. It is a subject which goes deep into practical religion: without belief in it, #emph[practical religion] must soon die in the hearts and lives of men.

For in proportion to the sense we have of our dependence on the sovereign God for all the good we need will be our esteem for him, our turning to him, our trust in him, our fear of offending him, and our care to please him. So too will be our gratitude and love, our delight and praise, when we experience and feel his free benefits.

In short, it is the very #emph[soul of godliness] to understand and acknowledge that all our springs are in him: the springs of our present grace and comfort, and of our future glory and blessedness. And it is to acknowledge that they all flow entirely through #emph[Christ,] by the efficacious influence of the #emph[Holy Spirit.] By “such things” saints “live,” and “in all of them” our spirits “find life.”

Such doctrines as these humble the minds of men, and so prepare them to exalt God. He has remarkably owned and prospered them in the Reformed world, and in our land especially in the days of our forefathers. We hope they will never go out of fashion among us. For we are well assured that if what we call the #emph[doctrines of grace] ever come to be despised or found distasteful, living godliness will languish and wear away in the same measure; just as these doctrines always sink in men’s esteem when serious religion decays.

We cannot help, therefore, expressing our joy and thankfulness that the great head of the church is pleased still to raise up, from among the children of his people, those who assert and maintain these evangelical principles, to supply his churches. We are thankful too that our churches (in spite of all their decline) still value such principles highly, and those who publicly own and teach them.

Just as we cannot help wishing and praying that the #emph[College] in the neighboring colony (as well as our own) may be a fruitful mother of many such sons as the author, by the blessing of heaven on the care of their present worthy #emph[Rector,] so we heartily rejoice in the special favor of providence in bestowing such a rich gift on the happy church of #emph[Northampton.] That church has flourished for so many years under the influence of such godly doctrines, taught them in the excellent ministry of their late venerable pastor. We hope that his gifts and Spirit will long live and shine in this his grandson, so that the people there may abound yet more in all the lovely fruits of evangelical humility and thankfulness, to the glory of God.

To his blessing we commit them all, with this sermon, and every one who reads it; and are

#v(1em)

#align(right)[Your servants in the gospel,]

#align(right)[T. Prince,]

#align(right)[W. Cooper.]

#emph[Boston, August 17, 1731.]
